{"primaryContentSections":[{"kind":"declarations","declarations":[{"languages":["swift"],"tokens":[{"text":"func","kind":"keyword"},{"text":" ","kind":"text"},{"text":"enumerated","kind":"identifier"},{"text":"() -> ","kind":"text"},{"text":"EnumeratedSequence","identifier":"doc:\/\/com.apple.Swift\/documentation\/Swift\/EnumeratedSequence","kind":"typeIdentifier","preciseIdentifier":"s:s18EnumeratedSequenceV"},{"text":"<","kind":"text"},{"text":"Self","kind":"typeIdentifier"},{"text":">","kind":"text"}],"platforms":["macOS"]}]},{"content":[{"anchor":"return-value","text":"Return Value","level":2,"type":"heading"},{"inlineContent":[{"text":"A sequence of pairs enumerating the sequence.","type":"text"}],"type":"paragraph"}],"kind":"content"},{"content":[{"anchor":"discussion","text":"Discussion","level":2,"type":"heading"},{"inlineContent":[{"text":"This example enumerates the characters of the string “Swift” and prints","type":"text"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"text":"each character along with its place in the string.","type":"text"}],"type":"paragraph"},{"code":["for (n, c) in \"Swift\".enumerated() {","    print(\"\\(n): '\\(c)'\")","}","\/\/ Prints \"0: 'S'\"","\/\/ Prints \"1: 'w'\"","\/\/ Prints \"2: 'i'\"","\/\/ Prints \"3: 'f'\"","\/\/ Prints \"4: 't'\""],"syntax":"swift","type":"codeListing"},{"inlineContent":[{"text":"When you enumerate a collection, the integer part of each pair is a counter","type":"text"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"text":"for the enumeration, but is not necessarily the index of the paired value.","type":"text"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"text":"These counters can be used as indices only in instances of zero-based,","type":"text"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"text":"integer-indexed collections, such as ","type":"text"},{"code":"Array","type":"codeVoice"},{"text":" and ","type":"text"},{"code":"ContiguousArray","type":"codeVoice"},{"text":". For","type":"text"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"text":"other collections the counters may be out of range or of the wrong type","type":"text"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"text":"to use as an index. To iterate over the elements of a collection with its","type":"text"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"text":"indices, use the ","type":"text"},{"code":"zip(_:_:)","type":"codeVoice"},{"text":" function.","type":"text"}],"type":"paragraph"},{"inlineContent":[{"text":"This example iterates over the indices and elements of a set, building a","type":"text"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"text":"list consisting of indices of names with five or fewer letters.","type":"text"}],"type":"paragraph"},{"code":["let names: Set = [\"Sofia\", \"Camilla\", \"Martina\", \"Mateo\", \"Nicolás\"]","var shorterIndices: [Set<String>.Index] = []","for (i, name) in zip(names.indices, names) {","    if name.count <= 5 {","        shorterIndices.append(i)","    }","}"],"syntax":"swift","type":"codeListing"},{"inlineContent":[{"text":"Now that the ","type":"text"},{"code":"shorterIndices","type":"codeVoice"},{"text":" array holds the indices of the shorter","type":"text"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"text":"names in the ","type":"text"},{"code":"names","type":"codeVoice"},{"text":" set, you can use those indices to access elements in","type":"text"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"text":"the set.","type":"text"}],"type":"paragraph"},{"code":["for i in shorterIndices {","    print(names[i])","}","\/\/ Prints \"Sofia\"","\/\/ Prints \"Mateo\""],"syntax":"swift","type":"codeListing"},{"name":"Complexity","content":[{"type":"paragraph","inlineContent":[{"type":"text","text":"O(1)"}]}],"style":"note","type":"aside"}],"kind":"content"}],"variants":[{"paths":["\/documentation\/swift\/uint32\/words-swift.struct\/enumerated()"],"traits":[{"interfaceLanguage":"swift"}]}],"schemaVersion":{"patch":0,"minor":3,"major":0},"metadata":{"title":"enumerated()","symbolKind":"method","modules":[{"name":"Swift"}],"roleHeading":"Instance Method","platforms":[{"introducedAt":"8.0","beta":false,"name":"iOS","unavailabl